                  "from": 182.64,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2780138,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "There, he met a pioneer in the study of global warming who suggested that carbon from the soil had moved into the atmosphere.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 202.77501,
                  "from": 194.935,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2780162,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "This is now looking increasingly likely.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 206.24,
                  "from": 203.68,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2780169,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "Let me explain. For millions of years, carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been regulated, in part, by a natural partnership between plants and microbes tiny organisms in the soil. Plants absorb CO2 from the air and transform it into sugars and other carbon based substances. While a proportion of these carbon products remain in the plant, some transfer from the roots to fungi and soil microbes, which store the carbon in the soil. The invention of agriculture some ten thousand years ago disrupted these ancient soil building processes and led to the loss of carbon from the soil.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2780170,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Let me explain.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 208.24,
                  "from": 207.04,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2780174,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "For millions of years, carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been regulated, in part, by a natural partnership between plants and microbes tiny organisms in the soil.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 221.365,
                  "from": 208.79999,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2780203,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "Plants absorb CO2 from the air and transform it into sugars and other carbon based substances.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 229.605,
                  "from": 222.08499,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2780220,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "While a proportion of these carbon products remain in the plant, some transfer from the roots to fungi and soil microbes, which store the carbon in the soil.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 241.19,
                  "from": 230.405,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              },
              {
                "id": 2780249,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "The invention of agriculture some ten thousand years ago disrupted these ancient soil building processes and led to the loss of carbon from the soil.",
                "meta": {
                  "to": 253.26501,
                  "from": 242.54999,
                  "speaker": "Speaker 1"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": 2780275,
            "level": 1,
            "value": "When humans started draining the natural topsoil and ploughing it up for planting, they exposed the buried carbon to oxygen. This created carbon dioxide and released it into the air, and in some places, grazing by domesticated animals has removed all vegetation, releasing carbon into the air. Tonnes of carbon have been stripped from the world's soils, where it's needed, and pumped into the atmosphere. So what can be done? Researchers are now coming up with evidence that even modest changes to farming can significantly help to reduce the amount of carbon in the atmosphere.",
            "children": [
              {
                "id": 2780276,
                "level": 2,
                "value": "When humans started draining the natural topsoil and ploughing it up for planting, they exposed the buried carbon to oxygen.",
                "meta": {
